window.NEO_FIELDKIT={"unit":"place-value","expedition":"The Counting House","tools":[{"title":"Place-value chart","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><rect x=\"8\" y=\"24\" width=\"84\" height=\"52\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#f3e6c4\" stroke=\"#8a6d40\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M29 24V76M50 24V76M71 24V76M8 42H92\" stroke=\"#8a6d40\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"18\" y=\"37\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">Th</text><text x=\"39\" y=\"37\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">H</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"37\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">T</text><text x=\"81\" y=\"37\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">O</text><text x=\"18\" y=\"66\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">4</text><text x=\"39\" y=\"66\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#b0412f\">7</text><text x=\"60\" y=\"66\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">3</text><text x=\"81\" y=\"66\" font-size=\"18\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">2</text></svg>","body":"<p><b>A digit is worth the value of its column.</b></p><p>In 5816, the 8 is in the hundreds column, so it is worth 800.</p><p>Columns from the right: ones, tens, hundreds, thousands, ten thousands. Each is worth ten times the one to its right.</p>","room":"The Front Hall","lesson":"Lesson 1 · What is a digit worth?"},{"title":"Ten-times slider","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"38\" width=\"80\" height=\"26\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#f3e6c4\" stroke=\"#8a6d40\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"60\" y=\"57\" font-size=\"16\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">3 6</text><path d=\"M58 22 H30\" stroke=\"#b0412f\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><path d=\"M38 14 L28 22 L38 30\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b0412f\" stroke-width=\"5\"/><text x=\"50\" y=\"86\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">× 10</text></svg>","body":"<p><b>× 10: every digit moves one column to the left.</b></p><p>45 × 10 = 450: 4 tens become 4 hundreds, 5 ones become 5 tens, and a 0 fills the empty ones column.</p><p>÷ 10 moves every digit one column to the right.</p>","room":"The Ledger Room","lesson":"Lesson 2 · Ten times bigger, ten times smaller"},{"title":"Placeholder zero","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><circle cx=\"50\" cy=\"50\" r=\"36\" fill=\"#e9d7a8\" stroke=\"#8f6a26\" stroke-width=\"6\"/><text x=\"50\" y=\"64\" font-size=\"40\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\" font-family=\"Georgia\">0</text></svg>","body":"<p><b>A zero holds an empty column open.</b></p><p>Three thousand and six is 3006: no hundreds and no tens, so two zeros hold those places.</p><p>Without them, 36 would mean something quite different.</p>","room":"The Strongroom","lesson":"Lesson 3 · Zero as a placeholder"},{"title":"Decimal ruler","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><rect x=\"6\" y=\"38\" width=\"88\" height=\"24\" fill=\"#d9b25e\" stroke=\"#8f6a26\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M10 38V54M18 38V46M26 38V46M34 38V46M42 38V46M50 38V54M58 38V46M66 38V46M74 38V46M82 38V46M90 38V54\" stroke=\"#3a2a10\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"10\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">0</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"80\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">1</text><text x=\"34\" y=\"30\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#b0412f\">0.3</text></svg>","body":"<p><b>After the decimal point come tenths, then hundredths.</b></p><p>Between 0 and 1 there are ten tenths: 0.1, 0.2, … 0.9.</p><p>0.7 is seven tenths. 0.07 is seven hundredths, which is much smaller.</p>","room":"The Measuring Room","lesson":"Lesson 4 · Beyond the point: tenths, hundredths, thousandths"},{"title":"Coin stacks","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><g fill=\"#d9b25e\" stroke=\"#8f6a26\" stroke-width=\"2\"><ellipse cx=\"24\" cy=\"70\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"5\"/><ellipse cx=\"24\" cy=\"62\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"5\"/><ellipse cx=\"24\" cy=\"54\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"5\"/><ellipse cx=\"24\" cy=\"46\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"5\"/><ellipse cx=\"54\" cy=\"70\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"5\"/><ellipse cx=\"54\" cy=\"62\" rx=\"14\" ry=\"5\"/><ellipse cx=\"80\" cy=\"70\" rx=\"12\" ry=\"5\"/></g><text x=\"24\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">H</text><text x=\"54\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">T</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"92\" font-size=\"10\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">O</text></svg>","body":"<p><b>A number is the sum of its parts.</b></p><p>500 + 30 + 8 = 538: 5 hundreds, 3 tens and 8 ones.</p><p>Each part goes in its own column, so nothing is written side by side.</p>","room":"The Coin Room","lesson":"Lesson 5 · Partitioning in more than one way"},{"title":"Comparing scales","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><rect x=\"47\" y=\"22\" width=\"6\" height=\"62\" fill=\"#8f6a26\"/><rect x=\"30\" y=\"84\" width=\"40\" height=\"8\" rx=\"3\" fill=\"#8f6a26\"/><rect x=\"14\" y=\"24\" width=\"72\" height=\"5\" fill=\"#8f6a26\"/><path d=\"M16 29 L6 52 H30 Z M84 29 L70 52 H94 Z\" fill=\"#c9a35a\" stroke=\"#8f6a26\" stroke-width=\"2\"/><text x=\"50\" y=\"16\" font-size=\"14\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">&lt; &gt;</text></svg>","body":"<p><b>To compare two numbers, start at the left.</b></p><p>3410 and 3089: both have 3 thousands. In the hundreds, 4 is more than 0, so 3410 is larger.</p><p>The first column that differs decides.</p>","room":"The Scales Room","lesson":"Lesson 6 · Which is larger?"},{"title":"Rounding line","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><path d=\"M10 60 H90\" stroke=\"#3a2a10\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><path d=\"M10 52V68M50 52V68M90 52V68\" stroke=\"#3a2a10\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"72\" cy=\"60\" r=\"6\" fill=\"#b0412f\"/><path d=\"M72 46 Q81 36 90 46\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#b0412f\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><text x=\"10\" y=\"86\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">340</text><text x=\"50\" y=\"86\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">345</text><text x=\"90\" y=\"86\" font-size=\"11\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">350</text></svg>","body":"<p><b>Round to whichever is nearer.</b></p><p>To round 263 to the nearest 10: it lies between 260 and 270, and is 3 away from 260. So 263 rounds to 260.</p><p>A number exactly halfway, such as 265, rounds up to 270.</p>","room":"The Clock Room","lesson":"Lesson 7 · Rounding: which is nearest?"},{"title":"Price tag","icon":"<svg viewBox=\"0 0 100 100\"><path d=\"M14 30 L60 30 L76 50 L60 70 L14 70 Z\" fill=\"#f3e6c4\" stroke=\"#8a6d40\" stroke-width=\"3\"/><circle cx=\"62\" cy=\"50\" r=\"4\" fill=\"#8a6d40\"/><text x=\"36\" y=\"56\" font-size=\"15\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">£3.45</text><text x=\"50\" y=\"90\" font-size=\"12\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#3a2a10\">= 345p</text></svg>","body":"<p><b>£1 is 100 pence.</b></p><p>£2.80 = 280p, and 75p = £0.75.</p><p>The same amount can be written in pounds or in pence: the digits stay the same; only the units change.</p>","room":"The Shop Front","lesson":"Lesson 8 · One number, many representations"}]};
